Synthetic inference checks only
===============================

Run this agent-owned portion from the repository root:

    python -m unittest research.ssq_dlt_conditional_inference.tests.test_adaptive_tree -v

The tests enumerate every positive-probability path in finite adaptive ternary
or binary trees, with depth at most six. They do not sample paths and use no RNG.
Node probabilities, conditional means, path masses and rejection/noncoverage
event probabilities use exact Fraction arithmetic. Boundary membership and
exponentials are numerical: the independent reference uses 100-digit Decimal,
compared with the module's 80-digit output at absolute tolerance 1e-65.
Consequently “exact enumeration” does not mean a machine-checked transcendental
inequality proof or an exact real-arithmetic boundary implementation.

The adaptive ternary law changes both conditional advantage and discordance
according to the previously observed difference. A separate null tree has
nonpositive conditional means at every node. The binary hit law also varies
with the preceding hit; a separate null law is bounded by the explicit p0.
These are visibly non-IID processes. For every prefix depth, tests integrate
the true-centered exponential and require expectation <=1, integrate actual
one-sided/two-sided noncoverage probabilities for the path-specific average
conditional mean, and check terminal p-value rejection probability in null
trees. At least one actual tail failure/rejection event is required for the
selected depth-six fixtures, so all checks are not merely vacuous wide bounds.
A separate compensated tree has positive conditional means in its first three
periods, followed by enough negative means to guarantee a nonpositive mean
only at the fixed sixth endpoint. It checks the broader endpoint-average null
without pretending that its early prefixes satisfy the per-period null.
Each prefix is assessed as a separate fixed endpoint; this test collection is
not a new optional-stopping rule or an unadjusted simultaneous coverage claim.

The paired wrapper is checked against a legal common-outcome binary pair that
realizes each difference; D=0 can be either 00 or 11 based on observed history.
The test verifies V=discordant_count/4 and does not split paired observations
into independent samples. Marginal H tests use [0,1], center=.25, and separately
exercise null_mean=.35. This checks that the centering constant is distinct
from the tested mean, and that paired and marginal scales are not confused.
Grid p-values are independently reconstructed with log(K), rather than using
the module's coefficient or its computed components as the reference formula.

All numerical method choices, alpha values (including deliberately large values
that make finite-tree tail events observable), centers and sample counts are
test fixtures. They register no grid, family, forecasting rule, power target or
prospective trial. Coverage of these finite trees is not proof of calibration
for all dependent laws; the primary supermartingale proof supplies that result.
Neither a passing test nor exact tree probabilities establish prediction skill,
provenance, a stationary mean, future performance or a persistent lottery edge.
The average conditional-mean estimand is allowed to differ by realized path.

No archive, lottery outcome, holdout, old result, model selector, prediction
ledger, external timestamp, network request or live forecast is loaded. A=0,
NO_EDGE and qualified prospective sample count=0 remain unchanged. Formal 12,
eight slots and approvals 01–04 remain UNESTABLISHED, as recorded by the user.
